1
00:00:03,231 --> 00:00:07,330
In the early hours of
Sunday, 31 August 1997,

2
00:00:07,481 --> 00:00:11,119
television in Britain and America
began interrupting its programmes.

3
00:00:13,451 --> 00:00:15,064
Diana, Princess of Wales,

4
00:00:15,215 --> 00:00:18,071
had been taken to a hospital
in Paris after a car crash.

5
00:00:18,562 --> 00:00:21,679
She was thirty-six years old, and
she did not survive the night.

6
00:00:23,851 --> 00:00:26,667
How she died was argued
over for more than a decade,

7
00:00:26,958 --> 00:00:29,123
until an inquest jury
returned a verdict

8
00:00:29,143 --> 00:00:32,019
of unlawful killing in April 2008.

9
00:00:34,211 --> 00:00:35,925
This film is about something else:

10
00:00:36,115 --> 00:00:38,571
the week between that first
bulletin and her funeral,

11
00:00:39,003 --> 00:00:41,559
as the broadcasters who
were on the air reported it.

12
00:00:46,731 --> 00:00:50,298
This is how BBC One told its
viewers, in the middle of a film.

13
00:00:52,190 --> 00:01:02,740
This is BBC One. We have interrupted our
programs for a news report. We now go over
to Martin Lewis in the news studio.

14
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:08,540
We interrupt this film to tell you we are
getting reports that Diana, Princess of
Wales,

15
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:12,040
has been badly injured in a car crash in
France.

16
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:16,640
French radio is saying that the accident
happened in Western Paris when the car she
was travelling

17
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:17,100
in collided with another vehicle in a
tunnel.

18
00:01:18,939 --> 00:01:23,387
In New York, NBC read out what the
news agencies were now sending.

19
00:01:25,248 --> 00:01:29,048
The latest associated press bulletin is
one eerie line.

20
00:01:29,048 --> 00:01:32,248
It just says simply reading off the live
wire copy here.

21
00:01:32,248 --> 00:01:34,348
She was 36.

22
00:01:36,192 --> 00:01:39,599
That morning, outside a village
church in his constituency,

23
00:01:39,619 --> 00:01:41,223
the new Prime Minister,

24
00:01:41,243 --> 00:01:44,300
Tony Blair, gave her a
name that stayed with her.

25
00:01:46,311 --> 00:01:58,311
people. She was the people's princess and
that's how she will stay, how she will
remain in our hearts

26
00:02:00,072 --> 00:02:03,752
That evening her coffin was
flown home to RAF Northolt.

27
00:02:04,223 --> 00:02:06,540
The Prince of Wales had
brought her back from Paris.

28
00:02:08,712 --> 00:02:10,967
The coffin was draped
in a royal standard,

29
00:02:11,338 --> 00:02:14,376
a sign that she was still to be
treated as part of the family,

30
00:02:14,787 --> 00:02:15,980
even after the divorce.

31
00:02:18,112 --> 00:02:20,764
Her brother, Earl Spencer,
issued a statement that day,

32
00:02:20,784 --> 00:02:22,300
and it was aimed at the press.

33
00:02:24,011 --> 00:02:28,961
However, I would say that I always
believed the press would kill her in the
end.

34
00:02:28,961 --> 00:02:33,961
But not even I could imagine that they
would take such a direct hand in her
death, as seems to be the case.

35
00:02:33,961 --> 00:02:41,961
It would appear that every proprietor and
editor of every publication that has paid
for intrusive and exploitative photographs
of her,

36
00:02:41,961 --> 00:02:49,911
encouraging greedy and ruthless
individuals to risk everything in pursuit
of Diana's image, has blood on his hands
today.

37
00:02:54,692 --> 00:02:55,696
Over the next days,

38
00:02:55,716 --> 00:02:59,659
flowers piled up outside the royal
palaces, and people kept coming.

39
00:03:01,732 --> 00:03:02,704
That Sunday night,

40
00:03:02,834 --> 00:03:06,433
ITN reporter Nicholas Owen
described Kensington Gardens.

41
00:03:07,065 --> 00:03:09,140
What struck him was how quiet it was.

42
00:03:11,111 --> 00:03:15,061
And you know the odd thing to me really is
how quiet it really is here.

43
00:03:15,061 --> 00:03:19,261
Yes, there's the noise of traffic, London
traffic going on as ever behind us.

44
00:03:19,261 --> 00:03:23,061
But in this park, you know, there's a
little bit of hubbub but not much.

45
00:03:23,061 --> 00:03:25,211
It's really very, very quiet indeed.

46
00:03:26,992 --> 00:03:29,552
The police left the park open
until one in the morning.

47
00:03:30,124 --> 00:03:31,520
Usually it closed at dusk.

48
00:03:33,532 --> 00:03:34,154
By Friday,

49
00:03:34,174 --> 00:03:36,441
ITN reported more than a million

50
00:03:36,461 --> 00:03:39,099
bouquets outside the
three royal palaces.

51
00:03:41,232 --> 00:03:42,306
Florists ran short,

52
00:03:42,477 --> 00:03:45,460
and some relied on shipments
of imported flowers to keep up.

53
00:03:47,431 --> 00:03:50,448
The royal family had stayed
at Balmoral, in Scotland.

54
00:03:50,979 --> 00:03:54,215
By midweek, there was open
criticism that they were out of touch,

55
00:03:54,686 --> 00:03:56,480
and that flags were not flying.

56
00:03:58,832 --> 00:04:02,090
On Friday, 5 September, the
Queen came back to London.

57
00:04:02,582 --> 00:04:05,940
At six that evening she spoke to
the country, live on television.

58
00:04:11,052 --> 00:04:12,628
She spoke from Buckingham Palace,

59
00:04:13,121 --> 00:04:15,339
and the broadcast lasted
about three minutes.

60
00:04:17,361 --> 00:04:21,661
First, I want to pay tribute to Diana
myself.

61
00:04:21,661 --> 00:04:25,361
She was an exceptional and gifted human
being.

62
00:04:25,361 --> 00:04:29,061
In good times and bad, she never lost her
capacity

63
00:04:29,061 --> 00:04:32,561
to smile and laugh, nor to inspire others

64
00:04:32,561 --> 00:04:33,811
with her warmth and kindness.

65
00:04:35,612 --> 00:04:39,059
She thanked the people who had
brought flowers and sent messages.

66
00:04:40,991 --> 00:04:44,941
This is also an opportunity for me on
behalf of my family

67
00:04:44,941 --> 00:04:48,541
and especially Prince Charles and William
and Harry

68
00:04:48,541 --> 00:04:51,341
to thank all of you who have brought
flowers,

69
00:04:51,341 --> 00:04:55,141
sent messages, and paid your respects in
so many ways

70
00:04:55,141 --> 00:04:56,491
to a remarkable person.

71
00:04:58,292 --> 00:05:01,250
She said there were lessons
to be drawn from Diana's life,

72
00:05:01,421 --> 00:05:02,955
and from the reaction to her death.

73
00:05:03,386 --> 00:05:05,000
And she made one last request.

74
00:05:07,001 --> 00:05:10,801
I hope that tomorrow we can all, wherever
we are,

75
00:05:10,801 --> 00:05:14,401
join in expressing our grief at Diana's
loss

76
00:05:14,401 --> 00:05:17,201
and gratitude for her all too short life.

77
00:05:19,001 --> 00:05:22,101
It is a chance to show to the whole world

78
00:05:22,101 --> 00:05:27,901
the British nation united in grief and
respect.

79
00:05:27,901 --> 00:05:31,001
May those who died rest in peace.

80
00:05:31,001 --> 00:05:33,701
And may we, each and every one of us,

81
00:05:33,701 --> 00:05:37,851
Thank God for someone who made many, many
people happy.

82
00:05:42,591 --> 00:05:45,448
The funeral was on
Saturday, 6 September.

83
00:05:45,939 --> 00:05:48,424
The timetable on ITV
put the departure

84
00:05:48,444 --> 00:05:51,240
from Kensington Palace at
eight minutes past nine.

85
00:05:53,212 --> 00:05:55,358
The coffin, draped in
the Royal Standard,

86
00:05:55,378 --> 00:05:59,500
travelled on a gun carriage of the
King's Troop Royal Horse Artillery.

87
00:06:01,332 --> 00:06:03,178
There were no military bands.

88
00:06:03,198 --> 00:06:04,924
Commentators said the crowd along

89
00:06:04,944 --> 00:06:06,920
the route was almost
completely silent.

90
00:06:08,631 --> 00:06:11,881
eyes fixed on the gates and silence will
mark this procession,

91
00:06:11,881 --> 00:06:27,231
no military bands, no music, no shouts of
command, silence,

92
00:06:28,972 --> 00:06:33,039
Bouquets from her brother and from
her two sons lay on top of the coffin.

93
00:06:35,212 --> 00:06:38,473
At Westminster Abbey, in front
of nearly two thousand guests,

94
00:06:38,885 --> 00:06:40,300
her brother gave the eulogy.

95
00:06:42,031 --> 00:06:45,181
to those at the opposite end of the moral
spectrum. It is a point to remember that
of

96
00:06:45,181 --> 00:06:51,681
all the ironies about Diana, perhaps the
greatest was this. A girl given the name
of the ancient

97
00:06:51,681 --> 00:06:59,381
goddess of hunting was, in the end, the
most hunted person of the modern age. She
would

98
00:06:59,381 --> 00:07:04,681
She wants us today to pledge ourselves to
protecting her beloved boys William and
Harry

99
00:07:04,681 --> 00:07:08,931
from a similar fate, and I do this here,
Diana, on your behalf.

100
00:07:10,732 --> 00:07:14,631
Among the guests was the First Lady
of the United States, Hillary Clinton.

101
00:07:15,263 --> 00:07:18,120
CBS News read out a statement
from her afterwards.

102
00:07:20,071 --> 00:07:23,721
Mark Phillips, thanks. The first lady of
the United States, Hillary Rodham Clinton,
issued

103
00:07:23,721 --> 00:07:29,721
a statement at Winfield House, the
residents of the American Ambassador Hill,
and it says

104
00:07:29,721 --> 00:07:33,821
in part on behalf of the president and the
American people, I came here to express
our

105
00:07:33,821 --> 00:07:39,021
deep sadness at the loss of Diana. She did
hard work in difficult places and she
softened

106
00:07:39,021 --> 00:07:45,321
arts and lifted spirits. We can honor
Diana's memory by continuing her work.

107
00:07:47,151 --> 00:07:49,686
Diana was buried later
that day, privately,

108
00:07:49,816 --> 00:07:52,932
on an island in the grounds
of Althorp, her family's home.

109
00:07:53,524 --> 00:07:56,459
Her brother had said her sons
could visit the grave in private.

110
00:07:58,431 --> 00:08:01,291
Millions of people who never
met her felt they knew her,

111
00:08:01,663 --> 00:08:03,299
and the Queen said so herself.

112
00:08:05,371 --> 00:08:06,175
Why did the death

113
00:08:06,195 --> 00:08:09,198
of someone they had never
met silence a whole country?
